# Line endings are part of the build, not an editor preference.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# `bin/console`, `bin/migrate.php` and the other six entry points carry a
# `#!/usr/bin/env php` shebang. If git checks one of them out with CRLF, the
# kernel reads the interpreter as `/bin/sh^M` and the script dies with
# "bad interpreter" before a single line of this codebase runs. That is the
# documented first command in README §7.4, so the failure lands on the very
# first thing a new contributor types.
#
# The repository content is therefore LF-only, for every platform. The Docker
# build, the GitHub Actions runner (ubuntu) and a Windows clone all execute the
# same bytes, so a bug cannot be present on one platform and absent on another.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` pins the working-tree form
# to LF even on Windows. Without it, every `git add` on this machine printed
# ~200 "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings, and the warnings were the only
# signal that the normalisation was pending.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything genuinely binary stays binary, so `text=auto` cannot corrupt it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary

# Generated by `composer install`; a diff here is never a change we want to read.
/vendor/**     export-ignore
/storage/logs/**  export-ignore
/storage/cache/** export-ignore
